  "interface_equivalence": {
    "parameter_quantifiers": "Family H01,...,H0m and m>=1 fixed before observing data. Under each evaluated P_theta, I0(theta) is a deterministic index set, even though it may differ between parameter labels. Local definition is applied separately under each law; no common probability space or parameter-measurability requirement introduced.",
    "measurable_rejection_rule_equivalence": "Given measurable rejection-set map Rcal into finite discrete2^[m], set D_i={x:i in Rcal(x)}; it is measurable as the preimage of the finite collection of sets containing i. Conversely given measurable D_i, define Rcal(x)={i:x in D_i}; for each J subset[m], its singleton preimage is intersection over i in J of D_i and over i notin J of D_i complement. Finite intersections prove measurability for every subset of finite target. These are inverse constructions.",
    "pointwise_counts_and_errors": "R=|Rcal|=sum_i1_Di and V=|Rcal intersect I0(theta)|=sum_i inI0(theta)1_Di. Both interfaces therefore have identical V/(R vee1), expectation FDR_theta and P_theta(V>=1) FWER_theta, including R0,V0 and empty true-null family.",
    "validity_and_scope": "Canonical52 permits empty nulls; BH preserves approved nonempty null family, a compatible special case. BH additionally assumes exact-uniform true-null laws and all-m mutual independence under every P_theta. Neither local definition nor owner substitution weakens those assumptions or asserts FWER control. Strong-FWER terminology from52 is not used as premise.",
    "example": "Under fixed evaluated law, I0={1}, uniform P1 and constant P2=0 give R2,V1 on P1<=1/2 and R1,V0 otherwise; both interfaces give FDR1/4,FWER1/2. Global-null comparison uses V=R pointwise, giving same rate identity in both definitions."
  },
